In Java, the method .intern() is used with String objects to manage
and optimize memory usage — especially when you have many duplicate string values.

Let’s break it down clearly 👇
intern() Method

intern() force karta hai String ko Pool me dalne ke liye.
Example:

String s1 = new String("Java");
String s2 = s1.intern(); // Pool ka reference mil gaya


🧠 What .intern() Does

When you call:

String interned = myString.intern();


It tells Java:

“Put this string into the String pool, and if an equal string already exists there, just use that one instead.”

So it ensures that all identical string literals share the same memory reference.

🔍 Example
String s1 = new String("Hello");
String s2 = new String("Hello");

System.out.println(s1 == s2); // false (different objects)


Now use intern():

String s1 = new String("Hello").intern();
String s2 = new String("Hello").intern();

System.out.println(s1 == s2); // true (same reference)


✅ Both now point to the same object in the String pool, saving memory.

💡 Why It’s Useful

Memory Optimization:
Avoids creating multiple copies of the same string in memory.

Reference Comparison:
You can safely compare strings using == if both are interned.

Performance in High-volume String Apps:
For example, when you’re processing millions of repeated values (like IDs, names, etc.), interning can significantly reduce heap usage.

⚠️ Important Notes

The string pool (also known as the intern pool) is a special area in the Java heap that stores unique string literals.

String literals in code are automatically interned by Java:

String s1 = "Hello";
String s2 = "Hello";
System.out.println(s1 == s2); // true (already interned)


But strings created with new String("...") are not interned automatically, so you need .intern() if you want to pool them.

🧩 Real-World Use Case

Imagine reading millions of rows from a database containing repeating city names:

for (String city : citiesFromDB) {
    city = city.intern();
    // now all "Karachi" strings refer to one shared instance
}


This can save a lot of memory when working with repeated data.